{% block title %}Who holds Sagamok's Waasmoowin roles? | RHT Circle{% endblock %}
{% block description %}Waasmoowin puts eight First Nations into two Hydro One transmission projects. Four Sagamok role lanes are visible, but the Nation's terms are not.{% endblock %}
{% block og_title %}Who holds Sagamok's Waasmoowin roles?{% endblock %}
{% block og_description %}What Waasmoowin is, who carries Sagamok's work and which ownership, borrowing and procurement terms remain unseen.{% endblock %}

{% block article_body %}
  <p class="lead">Waasmoowin is the corporate structure eight First Nations, including Sagamok, created to work with Hydro One on two major transmission projects and pursue a possible ownership stake in the completed power lines.</p>

  <p>The two projects are the North Shore Link from Sault Ste. Marie to the Mississagi Transformer Station near Wharncliffe and the Northeast Power Line from Greater Sudbury to Mississagi. Hydro One estimates the Northeast project alone at <strong>$1.8532 billion</strong>. That is the estimate for the whole Hydro One project, not Sagamok's bill.</p>

  <p>Through Waasmoowin, the eight Nations handle consultation, rights, employment, contracting and environmental work, and hold an option to invest in up to 50 per cent of the completed transmission-line assets. The Nations would have to pay for the ownership stake. Garden River is participating separately as a ninth First Nation project partner.</p>

  <p>The public record identifies four people carrying Sagamok's corporate, Council, development and financial roles. It does not show Sagamok's ownership share, borrowing, cost responsibility, procurement share or expected return.</p>

  <h2>Who carries Sagamok's Waasmoowin work?</h2>

  <h3>Angus Toulouse</h3>

  <p>Council authorized Chief Angus Toulouse to sign the first Hydro One memorandum of understanding in June 2023. Certified Ontario profiles show him as a director and Treasurer of both Waasmoowin GP Inc. and the Waasmoowin Opportunities and Consultation Council. He is Sagamok's filed representative on both corporations.</p>

  <h3>Michelle Toulouse</h3>

  <p>Michelle Toulouse is Sagamok's Director of Claims and Negotiations. On February 3, 2026, Council appointed her as the Sagamok Council representative to a "First Nation Board of Directors."</p>

  <p>She is not listed as a director of Waasmoowin GP or WOCC. Council made the appointment immediately after approving a new general partner for procurement, but the minutes do not say whether her board is that procurement entity or something else. The appointment record and organization chart are needed before any connection can be confirmed.</p>

  <h3>Evan O'Leary</h3>

  <p>Evan O'Leary is Sagamok Development Corporation's General Manager. Council minutes name him as the presenter of the Hydro One partnership structure, the early project updates and the proposed funding for community engagement, legal work and field activities.</p>

  <p>On February 18, 2026, Council authorized SDC to implement the amended Hydro One funding agreement. The public motion does not state what SDC may sign, spend, borrow or report without returning to Council.</p>

  <h3>Ryan McLeod</h3>

  <p>Sagamok Council minutes identify Ryan McLeod as the Nation's external Financial Advisor. The June 26 OEB filings for both Waasmoowin GP and WOCC identify him as their finance contact.</p>

  <p>The corporate profiles do not list him as a director or officer of either body. His Waasmoowin engagement, pay, authority and reporting line are not in the available record.</p>

  <h3>The SDC board</h3>

  <p>The federal corporate record, accessed June 7, 2026, lists Nicole Eshkakogan, Michael Abitong, Paul Eshkakogan, Leroy Bennett and McKenzie Toulouse as SDC directors. Four are current councillors. Council placed the Hydro One implementation work with SDC, but the public motion does not assign individual duties to those directors.</p>

  <p>These five directors are the corporate oversight layer. Members still need the board reporting record showing who oversees the work, who approves spending and what comes back to Council.</p>

  <h2>What members still cannot see</h2>

  <p>The people are now identifiable. Sagamok's commercial terms are not. The missing records would show what the Nation may pay, borrow, own, guarantee or receive, and how representatives are expected to report back.</p>

  <section class="news-article__questions">
    <h3>The records that would show Sagamok's deal</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>The signed Cooperation and Support Agreement and amended Multi-Project Capacity Funding Agreement with Hydro One.</li>
      <li>The Waasmoowin partnership agreements, ownership schedules and option terms.</li>
      <li>Sagamok's contribution, borrowing, guarantee, security and cost-sharing obligations.</li>
      <li>The legal names, ownership and rules for the separate procurement corporation and limited partnership.</li>
      <li>The appointment, pay, expense, reporting and conflict rules for Sagamok's representatives.</li>
      <li>The environmental commitments and the record showing how Sagamok's land, water, medicines, hunting and herbicide concerns were answered.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <h2>How Sagamok reached this point</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>June 22, 2023:</strong> Council approved a Hydro One memorandum of understanding and $20,000 for early project work.</li>
    <li><strong>July 27, 2023:</strong> Council accepted the 50-50 model while the First Nations were forming a limited partnership.</li>
    <li><strong>April 16, 2024:</strong> Council received a briefing that recorded proposed support from Hydro One, including $100,000 a year for an engagement coordinator for at least five years, $615,000 for engagement, field work and professional support, and $30,000 for a greenhouse project. The briefing said the ownership investment would not be required until the line was operating.</li>
    <li><strong>February 3, 2026:</strong> Council approved the Cooperation and Support Agreement and a new company for procurement work.</li>
    <li><strong>February 18, 2026:</strong> Council approved an amended capacity-funding agreement and directed Sagamok Development Corporation to carry out the business work.</li>
    <li><strong>February 24, 2026:</strong> Hydro One and Waasmoowin publicly signed their agreement.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The public signing described consultation, procurement, training and Indigenous-led construction monitoring. It did not publish the agreement.</p>

  <h2>Two Waasmoowin bodies are before the regulator</h2>

  <p>Waasmoowin Opportunities and Consultation Council handles rights, engagement, employment, contracting and environmental and cultural work. Waasmoowin GP Inc. manages the ownership option through Waasmoowin NEPL LP and Waasmoowin NSL LP.</p>

  <p>Waasmoowin GP and WOCC separately applied to intervene in the Northeast Power Line case on June 26, 2026. On July 2, Hydro One told the OEB it accepted both applications.</p>

  <p>The filings say each board has one representative for each of the eight Nations. They also say the two bodies have different mandates and interests and are subject to confidentiality restrictions and conflict walls.</p>

  <p>The certified profiles show seven of eight directors overlap and the three main officers are the same. Angus Toulouse is one of those overlapping directors and officers.</p>

  <p>How do the confidentiality and conflict walls work when the boards largely overlap? What information does Angus receive in each role? What can he report to Council and members? The governing agreements should answer that.</p>

  <h2>Where the two projects stand</h2>

  <p>North Shore Link is planned as a 230 kV line of approximately 105 kilometres. Hydro One's May 21 report records $26.3 million spent against an $82.3 million development budget and says a leave-to-construct application is expected in the third quarter of 2026.</p>

  <p>The Northeast Power Line is planned as a 500 kV line of about 205 kilometres. It is already before the Ontario Energy Board as case EB-2026-0154. The final Hydro One and First Nation partnership expected to own the completed line had not been formed when Hydro One filed its application. Hydro One said commercial negotiations were continuing.</p>

  <p>The OEB says its hearing does not decide environmental issues or the Crown's duty to consult unless they directly affect price, reliability or service quality. The environmental and treaty-rights record has to be followed separately.</p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>

  <p>Waasmoowin may produce ownership income, work, training, contracts and a stronger First Nation role in how the lines are built. The available documents establish those goals and identify Sagamok's main people. They do not disclose Sagamok's deal.</p>

  <p>That is not evidence the deal is bad. It is evidence the deal is material. Members need the agreements and a plain accounting of Sagamok's share.</p>
